okay today I'm going to talk to you  about growth of multicellular organisms  we're going to looking at cell  differentiation cell division and cell  elongation so first of all growth is an  increase in size or mass and we're going  to start off by looking at a process  called cell differentiation  differentiation okay  so before a cell gets a job before it  become specialized it's called a stem  cell so stem cells are unspecialized  then what differentiation means is they  become different they get a particular  specialized job so this stem cell here  that could become for example a neuron  it could also for example become a bit  of cardiac muscle in the heart basically  they can perform any they can become any  different type of cell which allows them  to perform a range of different jobs so  in our bodies we have lots and lots of  different types of cell that's a red  blood cell  that's a ciliated cell so cell  differentiation just means becoming  specialized so getting a specialized job  now this is really important because it  allows organisms to work much more  effectively if there's lots of different  cells that could do lots of different  jobs this differentiation can occur in  plants and animals to learn more about  stem cells please watch the video that  I've made about stem cells okay so  that's cell differentiation becoming  specialized so the second thing we're  going to look at is cell division so  cell division is essentially a process  where we start off with one cell and it  makes an exact genetic copy of itself  and this process can keep on going by  making many more cells the organism will  increase in mass overall so he starts  off for example with one cell there and  it will make two exact copies of itself  then these these daughter cells can also  copy themselves to make more genetic  coffees again so you can see this  process can go on and on to make lots  and lots of copies the process by which  this occurs is called mitosis again I've  got a particular video about mitosis to  show the different stages of that  because it involves just making exact  copies this also occurs in both plants  and animals but most of the growth in  animals normally happens when the  organism is it near the beginning of its  lifetime so in its young kind of younger  phase because when it reaches an adult  there's much less growth which occurs it  stays quite consistent which means this  cell division mostly occurs in order to  repair cells or replace old dead cells  plants however keep on undergoing  mitosis at the roots and the stones  throughout their lifetimes so the final  one we're going to look at is something  called cell elongation now this really  this only happens in plant cells not  animal cells so let's imagine we've got  a plant cell there cell elongation is  essentially when the plant cell can  expand becoming bigger so elongated  becomes longer it becomes bigger and  essentially due to this cell elongation  the plants can increase in mass and  their height so this is yet like I said  it's when the plant cell expands and  this only happens in plants plants only  not animal cells animal stem cells will  always stay the same size we just create  more of them whereas plant cells that  they are able to undergo cell elongation  so just this one on the right that I've  done in green that's the one that only  applies to plants the others apply to  plants  animals so those are the three different  methods by which we can have growth  occur in your multicellular organisms  the only other thing it's worth  mentioning is when this can go wrong and  when this can go wrong we can get cancer  now what cancer is is it's basically  related to this here it's related to  when we have cell division by mitosis  when this cell division happens  uncontrollably we just make more and  more and more and more cells but making  all of those cells we form a tumor now  that's really bad because a tumor can  invade and destroy surrounding tissues  it's really hard to treat as well cancer  because essentially it's just our own  cells so you can't take a drug to kill  these cells because they're your own  cells  you'd kill all of your other cells in  your body too so it's very hard to  target so just to summarize this is  about growth there's three main ways  that we have growth a multicellular  organism self differentiation which  means cells becoming specialized to get  a particular job cell division  which means making exact copies of cells  and cell elongation which only happens  in plants which is where the plant cell  expands and becomes bigger thank you  very much
 

